cff-version: 1.1.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
abstract: |
  Large HPC centers spend considerable time supporting software for thousands of users, but the
  complexity of HPC software is quickly outpacing the capabilities of existing software
  management tools. Scientific applications require specific versions of compilers, MPI, and
  other dependency libraries, so using a single, standard software stack is infeasible. However,
  managing many configurations is difficult because the configuration space is combinatorial in
  size.

  We introduce Spack, a tool used at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory to manage this
  complexity. Spack provides a novel, recursive specification syntax to invoke parametric builds
  of packages and dependencies. It allows any number of builds to coexist on the same system, and
  it ensures that installed packages can find their dependencies, regardless of the environment.
  We show through real-world use cases that Spack supports diverse and demanding applications,
  bringing order to HPC software chaos.
